{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778980823268,"sessionID":"ses_1cc82e6b8ffeZCRAO8D6mymYrV","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_k5GeRekEIGSf673URXN86At8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: /__w/eval-ont-agent-mondo/eval-ont-agent-mondo/terms/MONDO_0800180.obo\n@@\n-name: CPOX-related hereditary coproporphyria\n-def: \"Any inherited porphyria in which the cause of the disease is monoallelic or biallelic variants in the CPOX gene.\" [https://clinicalgenome.org/affiliation/40097/, https://clinicalgenome.org/working-groups/clinical-domain/inborn-errors-of-metabolism/]\n-comment: Per criteria outlined by the ClinGen Lumping and Splitting Working Group, the molecular mechanism (CPOX loss-of-function) was found to be consistent between the harderoporphyria cases and hereditary coproporphyria cases. In addition, the phenotypic differences between the biallelic and monoallelic cases appeared to represent a single spectrum of disease. Therefore, cases caused by inherited CPOX variants have been lumped into a single disease entity referred to as CPOX-related hereditary coproporphyria, with a semidominant mode of inheritance.\n+name: CPOX-related hepatic porphyria\n+def: \"A semidominant hepatic porphyria caused by monoallelic or biallelic variants in the CPOX gene. Monoallelic variants typically cause hereditary coproporphyria, whereas biallelic variants can cause the more severe harderoporphyria phenotype.\" [https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/issues/9703, https://clinicalgenome.org/affiliation/40097/]\n+comment: Per criteria outlined by the ClinGen Lumping and Splitting Working Group, the molecular mechanism is consistent across hereditary coproporphyria and harderoporphyria, and the phenotypic differences between monoallelic and biallelic cases are treated as a semidominant disease spectrum.\n@@\n-is_a: MONDO:0019142 {source=\"https://clinicalgenome.org/affiliation/40097/\", source=\"https://cli